WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.359 --> 00:00:04.240
Speaker 1: All right, let's start today with
a sort of comprehensive covid update.

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:10.519
You know, last week I told you that covid
cases were down in the United States on average,

00:00:10.519 --> 00:00:13.700
about 20 percent per day from their Delta
peak.

00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:18.570
And we laid out a few possibilities as to
why the numbers look that way.

00:00:18.570 --> 00:00:23.550
So to remind you, the things we talked about
last week were vaccination is responsible,

00:00:23.550 --> 00:00:30.340
more vaccination, fewer cases, OK, some natural
seasonality being responsible, even though

00:00:30.340 --> 00:00:34.829
it's not exactly lining up with the typical
flu season, that there seems to be some kind

00:00:34.829 --> 00:00:38.120
of peaks and valleys seasonality to covid.

00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:43.890
So many infections have gotten us closer to
herd immunity, combined with vaccination that

00:00:43.890 --> 00:00:45.260
has been suggested.

00:00:45.260 --> 00:00:50.110
And then also sort of the most nefarious explanation
is cases aren't actually going down.

00:00:50.110 --> 00:00:52.130
It's reporting has become less reliable.

00:00:52.130 --> 00:00:55.559
Some states maybe aren't even being honest
about cases.

00:00:55.559 --> 00:00:59.620
And then at the end of last week's segment,
we said, well, let's wait and see if this

00:00:59.620 --> 00:01:01.170
trend continues.

00:01:01.170 --> 00:01:02.399
The trend has continued.

00:01:02.399 --> 00:01:04.629
And in the last week, the numbers have dropped.

00:01:04.629 --> 00:01:11.580
Even more U.S. cases per day are down now
31 percent relative to the Delta peak.

00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:16.930
The Delta peak was about one hundred and sixty
six thousand cases per day.

00:01:16.930 --> 00:01:19.420
We're now down to one hundred and fourteen
thousand cases per day.

00:01:19.420 --> 00:01:26.369
Now, this is still like a wild, crazy number,
but a 31 percent decline is notable for sure.

00:01:26.369 --> 00:01:32.110
Now, last week, we didn't know whether deaths
were declining.

00:01:32.110 --> 00:01:38.200
And what I mean by that is that deaths tend
to lag cases by three to four weeks.

00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:43.190
And since last week, we were about three weeks
into the case decline.

00:01:43.190 --> 00:01:48.260
We saw deaths level out, but we just didn't
know our deaths really starting down.

00:01:48.260 --> 00:01:50.250
Are they on the way down?

00:01:50.250 --> 00:01:55.630
We now have another week of data and we can
see that, yes, deaths are declining in the

00:01:55.630 --> 00:02:00.850
United States, down about 11 percent on average
from the Delta peak.

00:02:00.850 --> 00:02:05.439
It's not that much, but it is now clearly
the direction.

00:02:05.439 --> 00:02:12.640
The Delta peak was about 850 cases per day,
roughly half of the thirty nine hundred cases

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:17.810
I'm sorry, deaths per day that we saw at the
biggest peak of the pandemic so far.

00:02:17.810 --> 00:02:23.290
So Delta peak deaths peaked at 1850 down to
sixteen fifty.

00:02:23.290 --> 00:02:29.330
Still a horrifying, unconscionable amount
of death, basically half of a 9/11 every single

00:02:29.330 --> 00:02:30.330
day.

00:02:30.330 --> 00:02:34.180
But you'd rather see the numbers going down
rather than up.

00:02:34.180 --> 00:02:40.160
Now, one other thing that we should be looking
at is whether what's going on in the United

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:44.530
States seems to follow or track what's happening
globally.

00:02:44.530 --> 00:02:48.331
If cases were going down in the U.S. but up
everywhere, we might say, well, maybe our

00:02:48.331 --> 00:02:52.010
reporting is wrong or maybe maybe something
just doesn't make sense because we saw, you

00:02:52.010 --> 00:02:56.310
know, Delta came to Europe and then it came
to the U.S. and things seem to sort of move

00:02:56.310 --> 00:02:58.640
in tandem, adjusting for vaccination rates.

00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:05.349
Of course, the U.S. Delta wave down thirty
one percent from its peak globally.

00:03:05.349 --> 00:03:11.260
Cases are also down about the same, about
30 percent decline in cases globally.

00:03:11.260 --> 00:03:18.350
And then very important as well, global deaths
since the peak of Delta also declining by

00:03:18.350 --> 00:03:26.300
about 25 percent, which you really like to
see now within the United States, we have

00:03:26.300 --> 00:03:32.530
clearer and clearer, clearer data about the
political alignment of this Delta wave.

00:03:32.530 --> 00:03:37.190
We've talked about this before and we continue
to get very interesting new data.

00:03:37.190 --> 00:03:42.190
Check out this incredible graphic, which I
will describe if you're not watching today.

00:03:42.190 --> 00:03:49.489
This is a plot of covid deaths per capita
against how pro Trump or pro Biden, a state

00:03:49.489 --> 00:03:50.989
was in twenty twenty.

00:03:50.989 --> 00:03:56.860
So if a state voted Biden over Trump by 20
points, it's more pro Biden than if Biden

00:03:56.860 --> 00:03:59.180
won by, say, 10 points.

00:03:59.180 --> 00:04:05.010
OK, now, if you're looking at this graph,
you can see that the states with the lowest

00:04:05.010 --> 00:04:09.860
per capita death rates as of September 2013,
14 day average.

00:04:09.860 --> 00:04:12.990
So we're talking now in September.

00:04:12.990 --> 00:04:18.359
You see that it's the heavy Biden states with
the lowest death rate, Vermont, Massachusetts,

00:04:18.359 --> 00:04:24.439
Maryland, California, to a lesser degree,
you know, places that are slightly pro Biden,

00:04:24.439 --> 00:04:28.479
like Illinois, Nevada, where Biden won, but
not by as much.

00:04:28.479 --> 00:04:33.419
The slightly Trump states are then doing even
worse in terms of covid death.

00:04:33.419 --> 00:04:41.409
So like Texas, for example, Florida, and then
you get into the absolute worst states with

00:04:41.409 --> 00:04:45.069
the highest average per capita death rates
in September.

00:04:45.069 --> 00:04:46.499
These are the heaviest Trump states.

00:04:46.499 --> 00:04:51.189
We're talking about Alabama, West Virginia,
Mississippi, etc..

00:04:51.189 --> 00:04:55.199
So as we've said before, as we've seen before
in the data, we don't just say it, we say

00:04:55.199 --> 00:04:56.199
it.

00:04:56.199 --> 00:05:00.009
If we see it in the data, trump voting in
twenty twenty.

00:05:00.009 --> 00:05:05.999
Predicts covid death rates now that vaccines
have been widely available for some time and

00:05:05.999 --> 00:05:09.069
that we know how you reduce the amount of
spread.

00:05:09.069 --> 00:05:10.909
This is not really a mystery.

00:05:10.909 --> 00:05:16.440
And when you look at the United States and
look at the least vaccinated states, it's

00:05:16.440 --> 00:05:18.009
essentially all red states.

00:05:18.009 --> 00:05:27.869
It's Idaho, West Virginia, Wyoming, followed
by Mississippi, North Dakota, Alabama, Tennessee,

00:05:27.869 --> 00:05:29.669
Indiana, Louisiana.

00:05:29.669 --> 00:05:34.319
You then get to Ohio, which is sort of, you
know, closer to 50 50.

00:05:34.319 --> 00:05:37.729
Ohio, a swing state in many elections at 54
percent.

00:05:37.729 --> 00:05:39.889
Vaccination, I think you get the picture.

00:05:39.889 --> 00:05:46.569
So the overall story here is cases and deaths
declining in the US cases and deaths declining

00:05:46.569 --> 00:05:50.430
globally, vaccination going up globally and
in the U.S.

00:05:50.430 --> 00:05:56.610
The states that are doing the best are the
most vaccinated states, which are Biden states.

00:05:56.610 --> 00:06:02.110
There's really no new information in that
sense, but this is the trend as of the last

00:06:02.110 --> 00:06:03.550
few weeks in the United States.

00:06:03.550 --> 00:06:05.419
So let's hope it continues.

00:06:05.419 --> 00:06:07.179
Let's hope it gets better everywhere.

00:06:07.179 --> 00:06:11.119
And we'll report back in another week or so
and see if the decline continues.

00:06:11.119 --> 00:06:16.080
If this decline were to continue in six weeks,
we would be an amazingly great shape.

00:06:16.080 --> 00:06:22.729
Caveat, I said the exact same thing in February
where we said if these declines continued,

00:06:22.729 --> 00:06:25.110
there would be no covid in like six weeks.

00:06:25.110 --> 00:06:26.430
That's rarely the way that it goes.

00:06:26.430 --> 00:07:24.819
So we'll continue to track the numbers and
see where we end up.

